WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.610
JEN PERSON: Welcome
to "Who's There"

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.059
a show about Cloud Identity.

00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:06.861
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.050
In the last episode
of "Who's There,"

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.290
I talked about why Identity
Platform is awesome

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.500
and how to enable it
in your GCP project.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.500
Today, I'm showing you how to
integrate Identity Platform

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.380
into your iOS app in Swift.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.900
If you haven't enabled
Identity Platform yet,

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.570
I recommend you check out the
last episode of "Who's There"

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.550
before proceeding.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.850
You also need an iOS app.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.200
In Xcode, start
with an existing app

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.460
or create a new Single View App.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.450
And if iOS isn't in
your jam, then check out

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.940
the "Getting Started"
videos for Android or Web,

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.840
both linked below.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.460
All right.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.730
With all the prerequisites out
of the way, let's get started.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.760
In the Cloud Console
for my project,

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.030
I selected the
Identity Platform tab.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.510
I previously added email
and password authentication.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.260
If you haven't added this yet,
click Add a Provider and Select

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.099
email/password.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.360
There are lots of other
providers to explore,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.010
but with today I'm just
sticking with this one.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.200
On this Providers
page, you see a link

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.710
in the upper right for
Application Setup details.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:19.970
This pops up a window with brief
Getting Started information.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.930
When I click the iOS tab, I
see "To get started with iOS,

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.570
you'll need to register it
and add Firebase configuration

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.410
to your app.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:29.180
Learn more."

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.670
I click Get Started with
Firebase, which takes me

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.320
to an entirely new console.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.580
Some of the information is
the same as the Cloud Console,

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.740
like project name and
ID, but then there

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.580
are all these new tabs down the
side that say Develop, Quality,

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.030
Analytics, and Grow, and the
big pop-up in the middle asking

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.880
about data sharing.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.927
If you've used Firebase before,
this probably looks familiar.

00:01:51.927 --> 00:01:54.010
But it's entirely possible
you've never even heard

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.650
of Firebase, let alone used it.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.350
Let's talk about
what's going on here.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.560
Firebase is a suite of products
to help you build your app

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.510
and grow your user base.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.850
It includes SDKs for
Objective-C and Swift

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.510
for iOS, Java and Kotlin for
Android, JavaScript for Web,

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.680
as well as several
frameworks, like Angular.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.940
There are specific for game
SDKs for Unity and C++,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.200
and so much more.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.950
In fact, I can make a
whole series of videos

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.240
just on Firebase.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.313
But fortunately, the Firebase
team has already done that.

00:02:28.313 --> 00:02:29.980
If you're interested
in finding out more

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.020
about any of these
SDKs, check out

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.900
the documentation and the
Firebase YouTube channel,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.070
both linked below.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.910
Now, in many ways, Firebase
is the perfect complement

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.560
to Google Cloud.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.200
Very broadly speaking,
Google Cloud products

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.210
focus on server-side
solutions, like

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.870
App Engine, Compute Engine,
Cloud Functions, Kubernetes

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.220
Engine, and Cloud Run.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.690
And Firebase products focus
on client-side solutions,

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.230
like authentication, cloud
messaging, Cloud Storage

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.330
for Firebase, and so on.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.760
I admit that it's
challenging to pin down

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.760
the relationship between
Google Cloud and Firebase.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.680
So my oversimplified
explanation is

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.560
that Firebase is the way
to access some Google Cloud

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.010
Platform products from
a client application.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.320
Specifically for
Identity Platform,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.510
this explanation works well.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.790
The Firebase Auth
SDKs enable you

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.370
to access Identity
Platform functionality

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.230
from your client app.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.310
With that in mind, let's head
back to that Firebase console.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.140
The Firebase console
automatically

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.610
opened to our project.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:37.690
Notice this UI indicates
that quickstart is already

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.310
a Firebase project.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.610
When adding Identity Platform
to a Google Cloud project,

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:46.450
a Firebase project is
automatically created.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.877
First, let's deal
with this pop-up.

00:03:48.877 --> 00:03:50.710
It's up to you whether
you want to check off

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.920
to use the default settings
for Google Analytics data

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.990
or select Do This Later.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:04:00.110
Some Firebase products depend
on analytics, like A/B testing.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.620
So if you decide to
incorporate other features,

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.360
you probably want
to enable this.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.060
Again, it's up to you.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.890
When you click on
the Project settings,

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.510
notice the settings
are identical to

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.790
the quickstart Cloud project.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.010
Firebase has basically
added some more details

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:17.529
and functionality to
the cloud project,

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:19.750
similar to when
you enable an API

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.590
or add software from
the marketplace.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.070
Since you want to add Identity
Platform to an iOS app,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:29.070
select Add App, iOS.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.500
This gives you a window with
a series of steps to follow.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.250
Keep in mind that the bundle
ID you choose needs to match

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.720
the bundle ID of your iOS app.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.720
The app nickname is
totally up to you.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.390
I won't provide an App Store
ID, since I don't have one yet.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.080
But if you do have one,
feel free to add it here

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.680
before creating the app.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.520
I'm now prompted to download a
GoogleService-Info.plist file.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.030
This file informs my iOS
app about my Firebase app,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.980
so I can interface
with Firebase products.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:07.350
Once downloaded, add
this to your iOS project.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.820
Click Next in the console,
and it gives the next step.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:12.600
Add the desired pods.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.530
So I will be using CocoaPods to
add the required dependencies,

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:19.587
but you can add the required
code without CocoaPods.

00:05:19.587 --> 00:05:21.420
Check out the guide
linked below to find out

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.230
how to install the
SDKs without CocoaPods.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.380
OK.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:30.160
Initialize a new pod
file and then open it.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.200
Add the Firebase/Auth dependency
and then Save the pod file.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.170
Close Xcode to prevent strange
behaviors that can rarely

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.090
happen when running CocoaPods
while the project is open.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.020
Then run Pod Install.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.510
Open the created xcworkspace.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:48.010
All right.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:51.940
In the project, in
AppDelegate, Import Firebase.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.400
This smart import will only
import the dependencies

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.580
you need, so you don't really
have to import Firebase/Auth

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.470
specifically, for example.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.270
In the
didFinishLaunchingWithOptions

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.580
function, initialize
the Firebase app

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.500
using FirebaseApp.configure.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:08.560
This initializes the Firebase
app using the settings provided

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.860
by the
GoogleService-Info.plist file.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:11.620
OK.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.780
You can now use the
Firebase/Auth SDK

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.750
to sign up, clock
in, log out, and get

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.770
user info about an
Identity Platform user.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.100
Identity Platform gives you
access to many different login

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.860
providers, including federated
identity providers like Google,

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.620
Facebook, and Twitter.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.500
You can enable several
login methods for an app,

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.090
allowing your users
to authenticate

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.160
the way that they prefer.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.040
Some login methods may
require additional steps,

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.740
which are listed in the console
when you enable that login

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:42.940
method.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.010
See the documentation
below for information

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.650
on how to implement
other login providers.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:51.040
For now, let's get back to
implementing email and password

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:52.750
login.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.390
Back in the Cloud Console,
create a fake user

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:58.240
with a test email
and a test password.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.690
Of course, you can also create
a new user from the client,

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:05.580
but this is a good way to get us
up and running really quickly.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.550
See how to create
a user in your iOS

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:11.240
app following the link below.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.020
Back in your iOS app in
Xcode, go to the viewDidLoad

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.570
for your ViewController.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:23.800
Call Auth.auth.signIn, and pass
the email and password you just

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.720
created in the console.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.570
This function passes
a completion handler

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.370
with two parameters--

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:34.230
an optional AuthDataResult
and an optional Error.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:37.480
AuthDataResult is the result
of sign-in requests containing

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:41.860
both the user and some
additional user info.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:45.690
First, I check that
AuthDataResult has a value.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.790
And if it doesn't,
log the error.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.810
Otherwise, I can do whatever
I need to do with the user.

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:56.010
Right now, I'll just
log that user's UID.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.990
Now, of course, my
example thus far

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.510
is not a complete auth solution.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:03.030
You need a way for users
to create accounts, handle

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.580
password resets, and log out.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.090
Each of these functions needs
some UI development as well.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:12.360
If you prefer, you can write
all this functionality yourself

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.220
using the Firebase SDKs.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.800
The related documentation
is linked below.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.000
But you also have the option to
use the FirebaseAuthUI module.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.440
This open-source UI was created
by auth specialists at Google,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.120
includes all of
the views you would

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.050
need to create an account,
log in, log out, and reset

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.030
password, which
you can incorporate

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:34.950
in just a few lines of code.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.799
And you can override the
ViewController classes

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:40.919
to change the appearance
of your login.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:44.039
Whether you make your own
UI or use Firebase UI,

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.220
you will very likely need a
way to know the auth status

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.200
before programming
any app actions.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.090
For example, you're
going to want

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.040
to check if there's
a user logged in

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.380
before writing to the database.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.540
That's where Firebase Auth
Listener comes into play.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:01.540
Let's look at how
to implement it.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:06.150
I create a function I'm
calling listenForAuthChanges.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.825
In this function, I
call Auth.auth.addSta

00:09:08.825 --> 00:09:11.690
teChangeListener.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.690
This function passes a
closure with an optional user

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.970
and optional error.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.490
Handle the error
if one is found,

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.740
then do what you
need with this user,

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:24.140
like assign the value
of a class's user

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.120
to the logged in user.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.440
For the purpose of this example,
I'll just log the user email.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.380
Then I'm actually
going to log out,

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.630
so you can see the
StateChangeListener closure

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:36.780
be triggered again.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:40.100
I put the code to log out in the
separate function here called

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:40.990
logOut.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.100
I run the app, and
the console logs

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:48.170
show that the user is locked
in and then logged out.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:49.580
Congratulations.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.730
You just added Identity
Platform to an iOS app.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:55.520
Ready to see what else
Identity Platform can do?

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.690
Check out the Firebase Auth iOS
guides, Auth quickstart code,

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.910
and the Firebase
YouTube channel.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:04.910
And be sure to subscribe to the
Google Cloud Platform channel,

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.210
so you can be the first to
know about new videos in this

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:09.867
"Who's There" series.

00:10:09.867 --> 00:10:10.700
Thanks for watching.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.242
And I'll see you on
the next episode.

00:10:12.242 --> 00:10:15.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

